<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Aro Confederacy</h1><p class="st-meta">1690 CE – 1902 CE (approx.) · capital Arochukwu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A trading network of the Aro people centred on Arochukwu and its oracle, which dominated commerce, including the slave trade, across much of Igboland.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Aro emerged at Arochukwu in the late 17th century from wars and alliances among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibibio-people">Ibibio</a> and Akpa groups, traditionally under leaders remembered as Nnachi and Akuma Nnubi. The new community controlled the shrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibini-ukpabi">Ibini Ukpabi</a>, an oracle that Europeans called the Long Juju, whose authority was respected far beyond Arochukwu.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>It was not a territorial state but a confederation of Aro lineages and settlements, ruled at home by the Eze Aro and councils of elders. Aro traders founded colonies along trade routes throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igboland">Igboland</a> and beyond, and used the oracle, alliances with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> groups such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abam">Abam</a>, and markets to move slaves to the ports of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bonny-kingdom">Bonny Kingdom</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/old-calabar">Old Calabar</a>, later dealing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The British saw the Aro and their oracle as obstacles to trade and colonial control. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-aro-war">Anglo-Aro War</a> of 1901-1902 British forces took Arochukwu and destroyed the shrine, and the Aro lands became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/protectorate-of-southern-nigeria">Protectorate of Southern Nigeria</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/aro-confederacy.jpg" alt="Aro Confederacy" width="320" height="457"><figcaption>Image: Ukabia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Aro_Confederacy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1690 CE – 1902 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Arochukwu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Igbo traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Igbo</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Arochukwu, Aro Confederation</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1796&amp;focus=aro-confederacy" aria-label="Aro Confederacy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4899 1976 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Aro Confederacy lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5219 2232L5094 2192"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5094 2192h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5219 2232h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5219 2232h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Arochukwu. <a href="/atlas?year=1796&amp;focus=aro-confederacy">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

